      learn: `<div class="section-title">What is Statistical Learning?</div>
<p class="theory-text">Statistical Learning Theory (SLT) gives us formal tools to understand <strong>when</strong> and <strong>why</strong> a machine learning model generalises from training data to unseen data.</p>
<div class="callout info">Key goals: minimise generalisation error, understand the bias-variance trade-off, and guarantee learning is possible.</div>
<div class="section-title">Core Concepts</div>
<div class="code-block"><span class="cc"># Empirical Risk Minimisation (ERM)</span>
<span class="cc"># Choose hypothesis h that minimises training error</span>
R_emp(h) = (1/n) * Σ L(h(xᵢ), yᵢ)

<span class="cc"># Generalisation bound (VC theory)</span>
R(h) ≤ R_emp(h) + √(VC_dim / n)

<span class="cc"># Bias-Variance decomposition</span>
Expected_Error = Bias² + Variance + Noise</div>
<div class="section-title">Bias vs Variance</div>
<p class="theory-text"><strong>Bias</strong>: error from wrong assumptions — underfitting.<br><strong>Variance</strong>: error from sensitivity to small fluctuations — overfitting.<br>The goal is to find the sweet spot.</p>
<div class="callout success">Rule of thumb: simple models → high bias, low variance. Complex models → low bias, high variance.</div>`,
