<p class="theory-text">PCA finds the directions (principal components) that capture the most variance in the data. It projects high-dimensional data onto a lower-dimensional subspace.</p>
<div class="code-block"><span class="cc"># PCA steps (from scratch)</span>
1. Standardise the data (zero mean, unit variance)
2. Compute the covariance matrix  C = (XᵀX) / (n-1)
3. Compute eigenvectors & eigenvalues of C
4. Sort by eigenvalue (descending)
5. Project: Z = X · W   (W = top-k eigenvectors)</div>
<div class="section-title">Singular Value Decomposition (SVD)</div>
<p class="theory-text">SVD decomposes any matrix A into three matrices:</p>
<div class="code-block">A = U · Σ · Vᵀ

<span class="cc"># U  — left singular vectors  (m × m)</span>
<span class="cc"># Σ  — diagonal matrix of singular values</span>
<span class="cc"># Vᵀ — right singular vectors (n × n)</span>

<span class="cc"># Truncated SVD keeps top-k singular values:</span>
A ≈ Uₖ · Σₖ · Vₖᵀ   (best rank-k approximation)</div>
<div class="section-title">PCA via SVD</div>
<div class="code-block"><span class="cc"># PCA is just a special case of SVD on the centred data matrix</span>
X_centred = X - mean(X)
U, s, Vt = svd(X_centred)
principal_components = Vt   <span class="cc"># rows are components</span>
scores = X_centred @ Vt.T   <span class="cc"># projected data</span></div>
<div class="callout success">PCA is used for visualisation, noise reduction, and speeding up downstream ML models.</div>`,
